      "status": "PASS",
      "started_at": "2026-06-26T10:00:57Z",
      "completed_at": "2026-06-26T10:02:11Z",
      "duration_ms": 74000,
      "summary": [
        "Reviewed 2 modified files (src/config/config.ts, src/tests/config.test.ts) plus supporting files (config.types.ts, slug.ts, json-storage.ts, config.dist.json). Full OWASP Top 10 evaluation performed. 0 Critical, 0 High findings. 1 Medium and 2 Low observations recorded — none blocking. Security sign-off: PASS."
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "security_issues": 0
      },
      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "risk",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:02:11Z",
          "note": "A09 / Information Disclosure — src/config/config.ts:235-238: The legacy-format error message for an invalid token type exposes the hostname key in the error string: `gitCredentials[\"${host}\"] must be a string, got ${typeof token}`. If application error output is captured in shared or forwarded logs, this leaks hostnames that have associated credentials. Recommendation: mask or omit the hostname in the error message, e.g. use `gitCredentials[entry #${index}]` instead of interpolating the raw key."
        },
        {
          "type": "risk",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:02:11Z",
          "note": "A04 / Insecure Design — src/config/config.ts (parseGitCredentials, new-format array path): No maximum length validation is applied to the `token`, `id`, `label`, or `host` fields. An adversarially crafted config.json with extremely long strings could cause memory pressure at load time. Low risk for a local developer tool, but a simple guard (e.g. reject fields exceeding 4096 chars) would add defence-in-depth."
        },
        {
          "type": "risk",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:02:11Z",
          "note": "A04 / Insecure Design — src/config/config.ts (parseGitCredentials, both paths): The `host` field is accepted as any non-empty string without hostname format validation. A malformed value (e.g. containing path traversal sequences, null bytes, or whitespace) could cause unexpected behaviour in downstream git credential matching (WP-005/WP-007 scope). Recommendation: validate `host` against a basic hostname pattern (e.g. no slashes, no null bytes, must resemble a domain or IP) in a future WP."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:02:11Z",
          "note": "A02 / Cryptographic Failures (defence-in-depth) — src/config/config.ts:300-302: saveConfigField() correctly applies chmodSync(0o600) after writing config.json on POSIX systems to restrict token file to owner-only read/write. This is a positive security control. Note: on Windows, no equivalent permission restriction is applied — acceptable for a local tool but worth documenting in the README."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:02:11Z",
          "note": "No hardcoded secrets, test tokens, or credential values found in config.dist.json or any test fixtures. config.dist.json uses an empty array [] for gitCredentials — no sample/placeholder token values that could be mistakenly committed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code-review",
      "status": "PASS",
      "started_at": "2026-06-26T10:02:37Z",
