      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "Maintainability — parseGitCredentials() is very well decomposed. The two branches (array path vs. legacy-object path) are clearly separated with inline comments, and the JSDoc is accurate and complete. The use of `as const` on the field array ['id','label','host','token'] is a nice touch that gives TypeScript a literal tuple and keeps the loop type-safe. No unnecessary complexity observed."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "Best Practices — The type assertion pattern `entry = value[i] as Record<string, unknown>` followed immediately by a runtime `typeof entry !== 'object'` guard is idiomatic TypeScript for parsing unknown JSON. The assertion widens to a working type while the runtime guard provides safety. This is the correct pattern for this context — no use of any unsafe `any`-based shortcuts. Consistent with how other parsers in the codebase handle raw config fields."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "Best Practices — ID deduplication logic (lines 188/231, `Set<string>`) is correct and efficient. The loop appends a numeric suffix starting at 2 — the convention is intuitive (matches common OS file-copy conventions like 'file (2)'). One observation: the suffix counter is not reset between entries, which is correct behavior. No bug here."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "Performance — No performance concerns in the new-format validation path. The O(n) loop over entries with a Set for duplicate detection is optimal. The legacy-format migration uses Object.entries() which is O(n) — equally appropriate. No redundant passes over the array. Config parsing is startup-time only, so even a quadratic approach would be acceptable for realistic credential counts, but the O(n) approach is clean."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "Future Context — The dual-format parser (new array + legacy object) is a well-designed migration bridge. Having the migration logic colocated with validation (rather than in a separate migration module) is appropriate given the scope — it keeps the parsing contract self-contained. As credential management grows (WP-005, WP-007), the decision to use GitCredentialEntry[] with a stable `id` field as the lookup key gives downstream consumers a clean reference contract."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "Future Context — The _defaultsCoverageGuard pattern (lines 37-42) is an excellent compile-time completeness guard. Any future AppConfig field addition will surface at this line if DEFAULTS is not updated. This is a proactive maintainability investment and worthy of being documented as a project convention."
        },
        {
          "type": "debt",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "Hostname leak in legacy error messages (Security Auditor Medium finding, lines 236 and 241): The messages `gitCredentials[\"${host}\"] must be a string` and `gitCredentials[\"${host}\"] must not be an empty string` embed the raw hostname into the error string. Fix-Forward was considered but not applied: the existing test at config.test.ts:379 asserts on the regex /gitCredentials\\[\"github\\.com\"\\].*string/, which would fail if the message format changes. This requires a coordinated Developer fix + test update. Recommend replacing `\"${host}\"` with a positional reference (e.g. `entry #${index}`) in both throw messages and updating the two corresponding test assertions to match the new format."
        },
        {
          "type": "documentation-forward",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "[documentation-forward] The _defaultsCoverageGuard pattern in src/config/config.ts (lines 37-42) is a valuable compile-time completeness guard. Add a comment in the config/README.md or inline at the declaration site explaining the pattern's intent — specifically that any new AppConfig field without a default will surface as a type error here, preventing silent omissions at runtime."
        },
        {
          "type": "documentation-forward",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T10:04:32Z",
          "note": "[documentation-forward] The {} empty object vs [] empty array asymmetry in parseGitCredentials() (undefined vs [] return value) is documented in JSDoc but not in config/README.md or any user-facing config guide. The next documentation pass should add a note explaining: 'omit gitCredentials or leave as null/{}  → treated as no credentials (undefined); use [] to explicitly set an empty credential list.' This distinction matters for consumers checking `config.gitCredentials !== undefined`."
        }
      ]
